%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: SAS Translation_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c7ddc960816fe0640a5beb89d4b167fe, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 1268832251904
    m_Localized: The beak and casque are made of silver, standing out against the
      polished wood finish of the rest of the wooden hilt. The base of the hilt is
      called buah pinang (betel nut), encircled by a golden pendokok or ferrule to
      connect the hilt to the blade. Such precious metals used in the making of the
      hilt and sheath imply that the keris is intended for ceremonial purposes rather
      than for duelling.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268870000640
    m_Localized: 'The elephant trunk design (akut belalai gajah) is commonly found
      on keris throughout the Malay Peninsula. It carries both aesthetic and functional
      value, as the sharpened edges above and below the trunk can be used to lacerate
      an opponent. Shorter keris are wielded in close-quarters combat, with every
      detail on the blade developed to give the weapon owner an advantage. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194944
    m_Localized: Indonesian traditions portray the naga as the ruler of the underworld
      and bringer of fertility, adorning gamelan musical instruments, appearing in
      wayang puppetry plays, and even decorating everyday objects such as the betel
      nut cutter. The serpent often appears with a pointed crown atop its head, signifying
      its special position.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194945
    m_Localized: "Historically, the number of lok in a keris represented the status
      of the weapon owner in society and differentiated between commoners, warriors,
      and rulers. To count the lok, start with the first bend on the opposite side
      of the naga\u2019s head. Can you see how many lok there are on this keris?
      Remember: it should be an odd number!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194946
    m_Localized: Batara Bayu is recognised by his large grin, his bejewelled headdress,
      and the vessel that he carries in his right hand. The multicoloured gemstones
      adorning the deity serve to elevate his position and match the stones decorating
      the full length of the sheath.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194947
    m_Localized: The sampir or the upper part of the sheath most likely features
      Bhoma with his fierce face, bulging eyes, and gaping mouth. Similar to the
      Kirtimukha motif in South Indian temple architecture, Bhoma is traditionally
      depicted above Balinese Hindu temple entrances for protection and purification.
      The use of ivory for the sampir makes this elaborate scene showcasing multiple
      figures even more prominent.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194948
    m_Localized: "The sundang hilt is traditionally made of hardwood and wrapped
      in natural fibres such as jute. This hilt, with its metal plating, elongated
      length, and large decorative cockatoo pommel, indicates the original owner
      likely belonged to the upper class. The angle of the hilt always conforms to
      the blade, enabling its wielder to maximise the blade\u2019s potential with
      a smooth arc."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194949
    m_Localized: "This sword hilt is covered with white same-gawa (stingray skin
      that has been dried and treated) and bound with ito (braided cords), creating
      the signature pattern of openings that reveal the rayskin underneath. This
      traditional double wrapping provides a rough pattern for the user\u2019s hand,
      ensuring a strong, non-slippery grip."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1268874194950
    m_Localized: The menuki or hilt ornaments under the ito wrap match the pattern
      of the sarute (buckle), fuchi (ferrule), and tsuba (sword guard). These sword
      fittings display the cherry blossom, a national symbol of Japan that was eventually
      used by the military as an insignia on their weapons. The menuki under the
      binding also features a family crest of three daimyo oak leaves within a circle.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 730108365877248
    m_Localized: Hilt
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 730108403625984
    m_Localized: The beak and casque are made of silver, standing out against the
      polished wood finish of the rest of the wooden hilt. The base of the hilt is
